!!Hattusa!!, also !!Hattuşa!!, !!Ḫattuša!!, !!Hattusas!!, or !!Hattusha!!, was
the capital of the [[ Hittite Empire | hittites ]] in the late
[[ Bronze Age ]] during two distinct periods. Its ruins lie near modern
[[ Boğazkale ]], Turkey (originally Boğazköy), within the great loop of the
[[ Kızılırmak River ]] (Hittite: //Marashantiya//; Greek:
//[[ Halys | halys-river ]]//).

French archeologist [[ Charles Texier ]] brought attention to the ruins after
his visit in 1834. Over the following century, sporadic exploration occurred,
involving different archaeologists. The
[[ German Oriental Society | deutsche-orient-gesellschaft ]] and the
[[ German Archaeological Institute ]] began systematic excavations in the
early 20th century, which continue to this day.[[ ^1 ]] Hattusa was added to
the [[ UNESCO World Heritage Site | list-of-world-heritage-sites-in-turkey ]]
list in 1986.

== History

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Hattusa_Yerkapi_rampant.JPG/250px-Hattusa_Yerkapi_rampant.JPG" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Hattusa rampart

The earliest traces of settlement on the site are from the 6th millennium BC
during the [[ Chalcolithic ]] period. Toward the end of the 3rd millennium BC
the [[ Hattian people ]] established a settlement on locations that had been
occupied even earlier and referred to the site as Hattush.[[ ^2 ]] In the 19th
and 18th centuries BC, merchants from [[ Assyria ]], centered at [[ Kanesh ]]
(Neša) (modern Kültepe) established a trading post there, setting up in their
own separate quarter of the lower city.[[ ^3 ]]

A carbonized layer apparent in excavations attests to the burning and ruin of
the city of Hattusa around 1700 BC. The responsible party appears to have been
King [[ Anitta | anitta-king ]] from [[ Kussara ]], who took credit for the
act and erected an inscribed curse for good measure:

<blockquote
  Whoever after me becomes king resettles Hattusas, let the Stormgod of the Sky
  strike him![[ ^4 ]]

Nevertheless the city was rebuilt, possibly by a son of
Anitta.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Hattusa_reconstructed_wall.JPG/250px-Hattusa_reconstructed_wall.JPG" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Reconstructed city walls of Hattusa

Around 1650 BC, the Hittite king Labarna moved the capital from Neša to
Hattusa and took the name of [[ Hattusili | hattusili-i ]], the "one/man from
Hattusa".[[ ^7 ]][[ ^8 ]] After the [[ Kaskians ]] arrived to the kingdom's
north, they twice attacked the city and under king [[ Tudhaliya I ]], the
Hittites moved the capital north to [[ Sapinuwa ]]. Under [[ Muwatalli II ]],
they moved south to [[ Tarhuntassa ]] but the king assigned his younger
brother, the future [[ Hattusili III ]] as governor over Hattusa.[[ ^9 ]] In
the mid-13th century BC Hittite ruler [[ Mursili III ]] returned the seat to
Hattusa, where the capital remained until the end of the Hittite kingdom in
the 12th century BC (KBo 21.15 i 11–12).[[ ^10 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Hattusa_reliefs1.jpg/250px-Hattusa_reliefs1.jpg" height="143" width="190"

  <figcaption>Reliefs and Hieroglyphs from Chamber 2 at Hattusa built and decorated by [[ Suppiluliuma II ]], the last king of the Hittites

At its peak, the city covered 1.8 km^^2^^ (440 acres) and comprised an inner
and outer portion, both surrounded by a massive and still visible course of
walls erected during the reign of [[ Suppiluliuma I ]] c. 1344–1322 BC. The
inner city covered an area of some 0.8 km^^2^^ (200 acres) and was occupied by
a citadel with large administrative buildings and temples. The royal
residence, or [[ acropolis ]], was built on a high ridge now known as
[[ Büyükkale ]] (Great Fortress).[[ ^11 ]] The city displayed over 6 km
(3.7 mi) of walls, with inner and outer layers around 3 m (9.8 ft) thick and
2 m (6 ft 7 in) of space between, totalling 8 m (26 ft).[[ ^12 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hattusa%2C_capital_of_the_Hittite_Empire_04.jpg/250px-Hattusa%2C_capital_of_the_Hittite_Empire_04.jpg" height="166" width="250"

  <figcaption>The Great Temple in the inner city

To the south lay an outer city of about 1 km^^2^^ (250 acres), with elaborate
gateways decorated with reliefs showing warriors, lions, and sphinxes. Four
temples were located here, each set around a porticoed courtyard, together
with secular buildings and residential structures. Outside the walls are
cemeteries, most of which contain cremation burials. Modern estimates put the
population of the city around 10,000;[[ ^13 ]] in the early period, the inner
city housed a third of that number. The dwelling houses that were built with
timber and mud bricks have vanished from the site, leaving only the
stone-built walls of temples and palaces.

The city was destroyed, together with the Hittite state itself, around 1200
BC, as part of the [[ Late Bronze Age collapse ]]. Excavations suggest that
Hattusa was gradually abandoned over a period of several decades as the
Hittite empire disintegrated.[[ ^14 ]] It has been suggested that a regional
drought occurred at that time.[[ ^15 ]] Signs of final destruction by fire
have been noted,[[ ^16 ]] but this destruction probably occurred after the
city had already been abandoned by the Hittite royal family, elites, and state
apparatus.[[ ^17 ]] Agricultural communities with a [[ material culture ]]
distinct from the Hittites settled in the remains of Hattusa as early as the
12th Century BC.[[ ^17 ]]

== Archaeology

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Yazilikaya_B_12erGruppe.jpg/250px-Yazilikaya_B_12erGruppe.jpg" height="164" width="250"

  <figcaption>Twelve Hittite gods of the Underworld in the nearby [[ Yazılıkaya ]], a sanctuary of Hattusa

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%E1%B8%AAattu%C5%A1a_B%C3%BCy%C3%BCkkaya_04.jpg/250px-%E1%B8%AAattu%C5%A1a_B%C3%BCy%C3%BCkkaya_04.jpg" height="134" width="250"

  <figcaption>Büyükkaya site in Hattusa

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Museum_Bo%C4%9Fazkale_13.jpg/250px-Museum_Bo%C4%9Fazkale_13.jpg" height="188" width="190"

  <figcaption>An artifact from the site of Hattusa is presented at Hattusa Museum

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Hattusa_magazzini_per_cereali.jpg/250px-Hattusa_magazzini_per_cereali.jpg" height="169" width="250"

  <figcaption>Large pottery at the excavation site.

In 1833, the French archaeologist
[[ Félix Marie Charles Texier | charles-texier ]] (1802–1871) was sent on an
exploratory mission to Turkey, where in 1834 he discovered monumental ruins
near the town of Boğazköy.[[ ^18 ]] Texier made topographical measurements,
produced illustrations, and composed a preliminary site plan.[[ ^19 ]] The
site was subsequently visited by a number of European travelers and explorers,
most notably the German geographer [[ Heinrich Barth ]] in 1858.[[ ^20 ]]
[[ Georges Perrot ]] excavated at the site in 1861 and at the nearby site of
Yazılıkaya.[[ ^21 ]] Perrot was the first to suggest, in 1886, that Boğazköy
was the Hittite capital of Hattusa.[[ ^22 ]] In 1882 German engineer
[[ Carl Humann ]] completed a full plan of the site.

[[ Ernest Chantre ]] opened some trial trenches at the village then called
Boğazköy, in 1893–94, with excavations being cut short by a cholera outbreak.
Significantly Chantre discovered some fragments of clay tablets inscribed with
cuneiform. The fragments contain text in both the [[ Akkadian language ]] and
what later was determined to be the [[ Hittite language ]].[[ ^23 ]][[ ^24 ]]
Between 1901 and 1905 [[ Waldemar Belck ]] visited the site several times,
finding a number of tablets.

In 1905 [[ Hugo Winckler ]] conducted some soundings at Boğazköy on behalf of
the [[ German Oriental Society | deutsche-orient-gesellschaft ]] (DOG),
finding 35 more cuneiform tablet fragments at the site of the royal fortress,
[[ Büyükkale ]].[[ ^25 ]] Winckler began actual excavations in 1906, focusing
mainly on the royal fortress area. Thousands of tablets were recovered, most
in the then unreadable Hittite language. The few Akkadian texts firmly
identified the site as Hattusa.[[ ^26 ]] Winckler returned in 1907 (with
[[ Otto Puchstein ]], [[ Heinrich Kohl ]], [[ Ludwig Curtius ]] and
[[ Daniel Krencker ]]), and briefly in 1911 and 1912 (with
[[ Theodore Makridi ]]). Work stopped with the outbreak of
WWI.[[ ^27 ]][[ ^28 ]] Tablets from these excavations were published in two
series //Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazkoi// (KB0) and //Keilschrift urkunden aus
Boghazköi// (KUB). Work resumed in 1931 under prehistorian [[ Kurt Bittel ]]
with establishing stratigraphy as the major focus. The work was under the
auspices of the DOG and [[ German Archaeological Institute ]] (Deutsches
Archäologisches Institut) and lasted 9 seasons until being suspended due to
the outbreak of WWII in 1939.[[ ^29 ]][[ ^30 ]]

Excavation resumed in 1952 under Bittel with Peter Neve replacing as field
director in 1963 and as director in 1978, continuing until 1993.[[ ^31 ]] The
focus was on the Upper City area. Publication of tablets was resumed in the
KUB and KBo.[[ ^32 ]][[ ^33 ]] In 1994 Jürgen Seeher assumed control of the
excavation, leading there until 2005, with the focus on the Büyükkaya and
non-monumental areas including economic and residential spaces.[[ ^34 ]] From
2006 on, while some archaeology continued under new director Andreas
Schachner, activities have been more focused toward restoration and
preparation for tourist operations.[[ ^35 ]][[ ^36 ]][[ ^37 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Hattusa_Green_Stone_2019-12-08.jpg/250px-Hattusa_Green_Stone_2019-12-08.jpg" height="338" width="190"

  <figcaption>The [[ Hattusa Green Stone ]], a monument believed to have religious origins

During the 1986 excavations a large (35 × 24 cm, 5 kg in weight, with 2
attached chains) inscribed metal tablet was discovered 35 meters west of the
Sphinx Gate. The tablet, from the 13th century BC, contained a treaty between
Hittite [[ Tudḫaliya IV ]] and [[ Kurunta ]], King of [[ Tarḫuntašša ]]. It is
held at the Museum of Anatolian Civilizations in Ankara.[[ ^38 ]][[ ^39 ]]
During 1991 repair work at the site a Mycenae bronze sword was found on the
western slope. It was inscribed, in Akkadian, "As Duthaliya the Great King
shattered the Assuwa-Country he dedicated these swords to the Storm-God, his
lord".[[ ^40 ]][[ ^41 ]] Another significant find during the 1990-91
excavation season in the "Westbau" building of the upper city, was 3400 sealed
bullae and clay lumps dating from the 2nd half of the 13th century BC. They
were primarily associated with land documents.[[ ^42 ]]

=== Cuneiform royal archives

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Treaty_of_Kadesh.jpg/250px-Treaty_of_Kadesh.jpg" height="336" width="200"

  <figcaption>[[ Treaty of Kadesh | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]]

Forty mercantile documents written in the Old Assyrian dialect of Akkadian
were found in the early 2nd millennium BC [[ karum | karum-trade-post ]]. By
the middle of the 2nd millennium a scribal community had grown up in Hattusa
based on Syrian, Mesopotamian, and Hurrian input. This included the usual
range of Akkadian and Sumerian language texts.[[ ^43 ]][[ ^44 ]][[ ^45 ]]

One of the most important discoveries at the site has been the cuneiform royal
archives of clay tablets from the Hittite Empire New Kingdom period, known as
the [[ Bogazköy Archive ]], consisting of official correspondence and
contracts, as well as legal codes, procedures for cult ceremony, oracular
prophecies and literature of the ancient Near East.
[[ One particularly important tablet | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]],
currently on display at the [[ Istanbul Archaeology Museum ]], details the
terms of a [[ peace settlement | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]] reached years
after the [[ Battle of Kadesh ]] between the Hittites and the Egyptians under
[[ Ramesses II ]], in 1259 or 1258 BC. A copy is on display in the
[[ United Nations ]] in [[ New York City ]] as an example of the earliest
known international peace treaties.[[ ^46 ]]

Although the 30,000 or so clay tablets recovered from Hattusa form the main
corpus of Hittite literature, archives have since appeared at other centers in
Anatolia, such as [[ Tabigga ]] (Maşat Höyük) and Sapinuwa (Ortaköy).

=== Sphinxes

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Sphinx_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg/250px-Sphinx_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Sphinx Gate entrance of the city.

A pair of [[ sphinxes | sphinx ]] found at the southern gate in Hattusa were
taken for restoration to [[ Germany | german-empire ]] in 1917. The
better-preserved was returned to Turkey in 1924 and placed on display in the
[[ Istanbul Archaeology Museum | istanbul-archaeology-museums ]], but the
other remained in Germany where it was on display at the [[ Pergamon Museum ]]
from 1934 until it was moved to the Boğazköy Museum outside the Hattusa ruins
in 2011, along with the Istanbul sphinx reuniting the pair near their original
location.[[ ^47 ]]

